I. Professional Core Positioning
This major focuses on "resource recycling" and "pollution control and treatment," studying efficient conversion processes for various resources (such as minerals, industrial solid waste, biomass, and recycled resources), as well as purification and treatment technologies for industrial wastewater, exhaust gas, and solid waste. The aim is to achieve a closed-loop cycle of "resources - products - waste - recycled resources" and promote the transformation of the chemical industry toward greening and low-carbonization.
II. Training Objectives
To cultivate comprehensive engineering technical talents with solid knowledge in chemical engineering, environmental science, and resource utilization, who can engage in research, technology development, engineering design, and operation management in fields such as resource development, environmental governance, and green chemistry. Specific requirements include:
- Master the basic principles of resource characteristic analysis and pollutant detection and treatment;
- Be familiar with resource recycling processes (such as hydrometallurgy and solid waste pyrolysis) and environmental protection engineering design (such as wastewater treatment and exhaust gas purification);
- Have practical capabilities and innovative thinking for solving complex resource and environmental problems;
- Understand industry-leading technologies (such as membrane separation, advanced oxidation, and carbon capture) and relevant policies and regulations.
